#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133531 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "cb31a55d-6107-509a-80e2-9bdffcaff6e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-5590" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $\\mathbf{b}$ shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac{2}{3}+3x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} + 3x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bc1e31e-5794-5ae3-8620-6bcaadde6a15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System With Collinearity"}] :question/id #uuid "c5097abf-c541-5c52-85d3-1e56a4e53cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5591" :question/problem "${-x_{1} + 4x_{2} = 1; 2x_{1} - 8x_{2} = 1; - 2x_{1} + 8x_{2} =-1$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. Find the general least-squares solution of the system $A \\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nsatisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\nA^T \\! A\\mathbf{x} = A^T\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -8 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 \\\\ -36 & 144\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ -36 & 144 & -12\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2}:=R_{2} + 4R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -36 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac{1}{3}+ 4x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} + 4x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133557 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/id #uuid "8a39f109-9436-55f8-bbb8-87253668378d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5963" :question/problem "${4x_{1} - 3x_{2} - x_{3} = 4; 8x_{1} - 6x_{2} - 2x_{3} = 2$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations and its corresponding normal equation shown above. What is the value of $a-b+c$?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write our system in matrix form as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe normal equation corresponding to\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{T}Ax &= A^{T}b \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 8 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}80 & -60 & -20 \\\\ -60 & 45 & 15 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x &= \\begin{bmatrix}32 \\\\ -24 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na-b+c=(-20)-(-24)+(-20) = -16\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9642ac63-6925-5ea3-925d-e697a6222064" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given in Matrix Form with Collinearity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86235d04-2f54-56c9-addd-095242f52c59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131991} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa3a517-2398-596d-8530-6e9ce8cf4426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131992} {:answer/id #uuid "4f271ffd-ca62-5430-9bda-65d6826ab1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131993} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9cf598-e066-51ba-8137-1d8ac71b82c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131994} {:answer/id #uuid "7438a4ee-7a1f-547b-9559-806b7e930ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9cf598-e066-51ba-8137-1d8ac71b82c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131994} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed53e70b-1aef-5e4b-b7f8-b4a7e0a45fc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131990}] :question/id #uuid "871a1be1-8f4a-5616-a788-c400e98cd9b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-40943" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax = b$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix} + x_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, x_{3} ∈ R$\nwhat is the value of $a + 2b + c$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d632ba55-0b60-52e6-be20-c84340c0fa37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132225} {:answer/id #uuid "fd996c99-8224-5079-9bbe-9b01e2197fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132226} {:answer/id #uuid "0ddeff2a-b870-5cf7-9269-a51e457fffb4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186132227} {:answer/id #uuid "16c9029b-cf7c-5215-8cad-b358ce